<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leif Erikson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 970–c. 1020</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Leifr Eiríksson, Leif the Lucky</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leif Erikson was a Norse explorer, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erik-the-red">Erik the Red</a>, the founder of the Norse settlements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>. According to the Icelandic sagas he sailed from Greenland around 1000 to lands he called Helluland, Markland and Vinland, making him one of the first Europeans to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. The sagas also credit him with bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> to Greenland. The site at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanse-aux-meadows">L'Anse aux Meadows</a> confirms a Norse presence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leif-erikson.jpg" alt="Leif Erikson" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sculptor Daniels, John K. 1875-1978 Photo: Sharon Mollerus · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leif_Erikson_Statue,_Duluth_(15290644106).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
